WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.490
The T-shape in front of you is Tiangong — in Chinese, 天宫, Heavenly Palace .

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:10.029
It is the second continuously-crewed space station ever flown.

00:00:10.029 --> 00:00:18.447
It is operated by the China Manned Space Engineering Office, and it has been continuously occupied since June 2022.

00:00:18.447 --> 00:00:19.545
Drag to rotate.

00:00:19.545 --> 00:00:21.376
You'll see three modules.

00:00:21.376 --> 00:00:29.574
The core of the T is Tianhe — meaning Harmony of the Heavens — launched in April 2021 on a Long March 5B rocket.

00:00:29.574 --> 00:00:36.822
Tianhe holds the crew quarters, the life-support systems, the control cabin, and the docking ports.

00:00:36.822 --> 00:00:39.457
Click Wentian — the arm to one side.

00:00:39.457 --> 00:00:46.338
Wentian — Quest for the Heavens — is the first experimental laboratory, launched in July 2022.

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:51.536
It houses life-science experiments and the airlock used for spacewalks.

00:00:51.536 --> 00:00:54.683
Click Mengtian — the arm to the other side.

00:00:54.683 --> 00:01:01.125
Mengtian — Dreaming of the Heavens — is the second laboratory, launched in October 2022.

00:01:01.125 --> 00:01:06.835
It carries physics experiments and a cold-atom clock for fundamental research.

00:01:06.835 --> 00:01:08.665
Look at the solar arrays.

00:01:08.665 --> 00:01:12.472
Four large panels, two at each lab module's far end.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:17.596
Together they produce about one hundred kilowatts of electrical power.

00:01:17.596 --> 00:01:21.183
Look at the docking ports on the front of Tianhe.

00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:31.725
Crews arrive on Shenzhou spacecraft — the Chinese crewed vehicle, derived in part from Soyuz but with a domestically developed re-entry capsule.

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:36.410
Cargo arrives on Tianzhou ships — the Chinese resupply vehicles.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.557
Tiangong was assembled in seventeen months.

00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:45.340
By comparison, the International Space Station was assembled over twelve years.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:54.198
Tiangong is smaller — about a sixth the mass of the ISS — but it was built on a much tighter timeline by a single agency.

00:01:54.198 --> 00:01:55.443
Click any module.

00:01:55.443 --> 00:02:02.177
The right panel opens with its builder, launch vehicle, launch date, and pressurized volume.

00:02:02.177 --> 00:02:03.202
Three modules.

00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:04.007
One agency.

00:02:04.007 --> 00:02:06.570
Continuously crewed for four years.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:14.256
Most people who ask "is there anyone else in space" don't yet know to include the answer: yes, also here.

